+# Decision: Preview Cache Eviction Policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker's private cache holds rendered JPEG contact sheets up to a 12 GiB bound and must evict entries to stay under it. The workload mixes two access patterns unevenly across productions: long sequential screenings that touch many sheets exactly once, and comparison sessions that repeatedly revisit a smaller working set. An eviction policy that tracks only recency treats both patterns the same way, so a long screening can flush sheets a comparison session is actively relying on.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+- **Protection of repeatedly revisited sheets from a long sequential screening** (major) — the specific failure mode the cache is most exposed to, given the workload described above.
+- **Bookkeeping and runtime cost** (moderate) — ongoing work the policy adds beyond tracking a single queue.
+- **Tuning burden** (moderate) — how much the policy needs a human to set and keep calibrated as workload shifts.
+- **Adaptation speed when the working set changes** (minor) — e.g., an editor switching projects, so yesterday's reused sheets stop mattering.
+- **Risk of retaining stale favorites past their usefulness** (minor) — the mirror of adaptation speed, from the side of over-retention rather than under-retention.
+
+No project tenet names this decision directly; it is a local workload/performance trade-off. It is consistent with the HLD goal that the request/response contract hold "for every eviction policy," and with the tenet that replacement bookkeeping stays private to the preview worker — both are satisfied identically by all three options below, so neither discriminates between them.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+A single queue ordered by recency; every admission and every hit moves the entry to the most-recently-used position, and eviction takes from the least-recently-used end.
+
+- Protection of revisited sheets (major): **weak**. Recency alone doesn't distinguish a sheet seen once from one seen repeatedly — a long sequential screening advances every fresh sheet to the front in turn, aging a comparison sheet toward eviction purely because wall-clock time passed, not because it stopped being useful.
+- Bookkeeping/runtime cost (moderate): **strong**. One queue, one O(1) move per access, no counters or background passes.
+- Tuning burden (moderate): **strong**. No parameters beyond the existing 12 GiB bound.
+- Adaptation speed (minor): **strong**. Recency reflects current work immediately; nothing lingers past its last access for long.
+- Stale-favorite risk (minor): **strong**. Nothing is retained once it stops being accessed.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+Each entry carries an access counter; counters decay on a periodic aging pass; eviction takes the entry with the lowest aged count.
+
+- Protection of revisited sheets (major): **partial**. A sheet that accumulates enough repeat hits resists eviction, but protection is probabilistic rather than structural — during the screening itself, a comparison sheet needs enough hits to outweigh the volume of one-off sheets flooding in before its count is high enough to matter.
+- Bookkeeping/runtime cost (moderate): **weak**. Counter increments on every access plus a periodic aging pass are ongoing cost beyond a single queue.
+- Tuning burden (moderate): **weak**. The aging rate and period are themselves parameters to calibrate, a comparable burden to 2Q's size split.
+- Adaptation speed (minor): **weak**. A count built up on a previous project's clips keeps them warm until the aging pass decays them, lagging a project switch.
+- Stale-favorite risk (minor): **weak**. This is the policy's named failure mode — yesterday's favorites can outlast their usefulness until aging catches up.
+
+### 2Q
+
+Two queues: a once-seen queue for sheets admitted but not yet re-requested, and a reused queue for sheets requested more than once. Eviction draws from once-seen first, falling back to the reused queue only once once-seen is empty.
+
+- Protection of revisited sheets (major): **strong**. The separation is structural, not probabilistic — a single hit promotes a sheet out of the once-seen queue, so it cannot be evicted by a sequential pass through fresh sheets regardless of how large that pass is.
+- Bookkeeping/runtime cost (moderate): **partial**. Two queues and a reclassification on each hit, but no counters or aging passes to run in the background.
+- Tuning burden (moderate): **partial**. Needs a size-split parameter between the two queues, but it is one value rather than frequency-aging's ongoing rate/period calibration.
+- Adaptation speed (minor): **partial**. A sheet from an abandoned project sits in the reused queue until it's displaced by newer repeat traffic, which is slower than LRU's immediate response but still bounded by the reused queue's own size rather than open-ended.
+- Stale-favorite risk (minor): **partial**. Bounded by the reused queue's size limit rather than a decay timer — a stale favorite is eventually displaced once other sheets earn repeat traffic, but not as promptly as LRU drops an unused entry.
+
+## Selection
+
+2Q is chosen. The major criterion — protecting revisited sheets from a long sequential screening — is the failure mode this cache is most exposed to given the production workload, and 2Q is the only option that satisfies it structurally rather than probabilistically or not at all. The moderate criteria (bookkeeping cost, tuning burden) are traded away deliberately: two queues and a size-split parameter are accepted as the cost of making the major criterion's protection structural rather than best-effort.
+
+This commits the design to building and maintaining two queues and forecloses on ever needing a counter-aging background pass. It obligates a follow-up tuning decision — the size split between the two queues, tracked as an open question in the LLD — once real usage data is available to set it against.
