 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses 2Q. Two queues track cache entries, both private to this worker: a once-seen queue holding sheets admitted but not yet re-requested, and a reused queue holding sheets that have been requested more than once. A newly admitted sheet enters the once-seen queue. A request that hits a sheet already tracked by either queue promotes it into the reused queue. Eviction draws from the once-seen queue first and falls back to the reused queue only once the once-seen queue is empty, so a single long sequential screening — which fills the once-seen queue with sheets requested exactly once — cannot evict sheets that comparison work has already revisited. The size split between the two queues is a tunable parameter (see Open Questions & Future Decisions).
+
+None of LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, or 2Q changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget — see Decisions & Alternatives.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q (once-seen queue + reused queue) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | See [decisions/eviction-policy.md](decisions/eviction-policy.md). |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. The size split between the once-seen and reused queues is unset. Pick a starting split and a method for tuning it (fixed ratio vs. workload-driven) during implementation.
